  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nWhile reviewing WebKit's implementation of processing instructions and `<template for>` (https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/73326) I ran into four problems with how the parser handles them.\n\n#### 1. U+0000 in processing instruction data\n\nThe [processing instruction data state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#processing-instruction-data-state) appends U+0000 as-is. Other states that build up token data, such as the comment and attribute value states, replace it with U+FFFD, as does the [bogus comment state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#bogus-comment-state) that handles U+0000 in a target. The WebKit patch already produces U+FFFD here.\n\nI suggest adding this to the processing instruction data state:\n\n> U+0000 NULL: This is an [unexpected-null-character](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#parse-error-unexpected-null-character) parse error. Append a U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER character to the current processing instruction token's data.\n\nThe questionable state reconsumes in the data state, so it needs no entry of its own. [processing-instructions.dat](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/html/syntax/parsing/resources/processing-instructions.dat) has no U+0000 case yet.\n\n#### 2. End of file inside `<template for>` leaves the markers in place\n\nThe marker cleanup (\"If template's insertion target is not null: … remove start … remove end\") is only part of the `</template>` steps in [\"in head\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#parsing-main-inhead). The end-of-file token in [\"in template\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#parsing-main-intemplate) pops the template without it. Given this input, with nothing after it:\n\n```html\n<div id=target><?start name=\"p\">Old<?end></div>\n<template for=p>New\n```\n\nthe result is `<div id=\"target\"><?start name=\"p\"?>New<?end ?></div>`. The WebKit patch removes the markers, giving `<div id=\"target\">New</div>`.\n\nI suggest moving the cleanup into its own algorithm and running it from the end-of-file steps too. There's no WPT for this yet.\n\n#### 3. Script during \"prepare content patching\" can write into the tokenizer or blow away the document\n\n[Prepare content patching](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#prepare-content-patching) removes the nodes between the markers while the parser is in the middle of the template start tag. As its note says, that can fire `pagehide`, and microtasks run after the handler. Nothing stops that script from calling `document.write()` or `document.open()`:\n\n```html\n<div id=target><?start name=\"p\"><iframe></iframe><?end></div>\n<script>\n  document.querySelector(\"iframe\").contentWindow.onpagehide = () => document.write(\"<p>Hi\");\n</script>\n<template for=p>New</template>\n```\n\nThe insertion point is undefined here, so `document.write()` runs the [document open steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dynamic-markup-insertion.html#document-open-steps) and replaces all children of the document mid-token. If the `<template for>` was itself written by `document.write()`, the insertion point is defined and the tokenizer is re-entered while tree construction is still processing the start tag.\n\n[Create an element for a token](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#create-an-element-for-the-token) prevents this for custom element constructors by incrementing the [throw-on-dynamic-markup-insertion counter](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dynamic-markup-insertion.html#throw-on-dynamic-markup-insertion-counter). I suggest prepare content patching increments scope's node document's counter around its removal loop, and that the counter's description mentions this. The WebKit patch doesn't guard against this either.\n\n#### 4. The \"scope is a template element\" step and its note\n\nThe `for` branch of the template start tag steps in [\"in head\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#parsing-main-inhead) says:\n\n> If scope is a template element, then set scope to scope's template contents.\n>\n> Note: … Nested patching is not supported, since in this case the template element's template contents will have no children and prepare content patching will fail.\n\nThe [appropriate place for inserting a node](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#appropriate-place-for-inserting-a-node) doesn't return a template as the target parent; it substitutes the template's contents or its insertion target. Scope can therefore only be a template element if a patch's insertion target is itself one, which takes script putting a marker directly into a template's children. For a `<template for>` inside another, scope is the outer template's insertion target, and nested patching works:\n\n```html\n<div id=host><?start name=\"outer\">old<?end></div>\n<template for=outer>before<?marker name=\"inner\">after<template for=inner>PATCHED</template></template>\n```\n\nThis produces \"beforePATCHEDafter\". [template-for-nested-patch.html](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/html/semantics/scripting-1/the-template-element/template-for/template-for-nested-patch.html) expects that and asks the editors to confirm; the WebKit patch produces it as well.\n\nI suggest dropping the step and rewriting the note to say patching works inside template contents, declarative shadow roots, and nested `<template for>`. While there, \"the node that contains the adjusted insertion location\" could become adjustedInsertionLocation's target parent, matching the insertion location tuples from #12831.\n\ncc @foolip @noamr @whatwg/html-parser\n",
